# build output. The glob matters: the packaging scripts each use their own
# directory -- build-mac, build-win, build-linux -- so that packaging never
# disturbs the incremental build in build/. Without the glob those three land
# in `git add -A`, which drags in CMake's _deps/ and, with it, whole embedded
# git repositories.
/build/
/build-*/
/dist/
# cgo build artifacts. Anchored with a leading slash: a gitignore pattern
# with no slash in it matches at ANY depth, and the bare form of the last two
# lines silently swallowed include/omegassh/omegassh.h -- the hand-written
# contract header every consumer includes -- so it was never committed and a
# fresh clone could not build the C++ side at all.
/libomegassh.a
/libomegassh.h
/libomegassh.so
/libomegassh.dylib
/omegassh.lib
/omegassh.h

# NOT ignored: third_party/sqlite3/. The amalgamation is committed on
# purpose -- "one .c file, no package manager" only pays off if a fresh
# clone builds without a download step, and pinning the exact source we
# ship credentials on top of is the point. Delete these two lines and add
# the directory here if that call is ever reversed.

# staging directories the Windows c-archive probe copies sources into
/tests/win/minarchive/_stage*/

# lab fixtures generated by tests/labsshd.sh
/tests/.lab/

# editors
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp

# Derived artwork. art/omega-icon.svg is the source and IS committed; these are
# what art/make-icons.py and iconutil produce from it.
#
# Omega.iconset/ and the PNGs are NOT ignored, deliberately: a checkout that
# cannot build an icon without installing cairosvg is a checkout where
# packaging needs a Python toolchain. The .icns is the exception because
# iconutil is macOS-only, so it cannot be regenerated everywhere anyway.
/art/Omega.icns
/art/_master.png

# OS
.DS_Store